Dynamic Array (ArrayList): 

1. Introduction
	->A Dynamic Array is a linear data structure whose size can grow automatically at runtime.
	->It stores elements in continuous memory, just like a normal array.
	->In Java, the ArrayList class (in java.util, Collections Framework) is the implementation of a Dynamic Array.

2. Why do we need it? (Problem with a normal array)
	
	->A normal array has a fixed size, decided at creation (int[] a = new int[5]).
	->If we need to store more elements, we cannot extend it. We must create a new array manually and copy the data.
	->If we create a very big array “just in case”, memory is wasted.
	->A Dynamic Array solves this by resizing itself automatically when it becomes full.
3. Important Terms
	
	->Size: number of elements currently stored in arrayList.
	->Capacity: maximum number of elements the internal array can store(hold) before resizing.
	->Initial ArrayList capacity is 10
	->ArrayList  growing capacity formula:
			New Capacity = Old Capacity + (Old Capacity / 2)
	->Rule: size <= capacity always.
	->Example: elements 10, 20, 30, 40 in an array of 10 boxes means size = 4, capacity = 10.
	
4. Internal Structure

	->Internally, ArrayList keeps an Object[] array plus an int size variable.
	->add() puts the element at index size and then does size++.


5. Working of Dynamic Array (Step by Step)

	->Create a simple array with a starting capacity and size = 0.
		arra=>	|   |  |   |	   |	   |		|   |  |   |	   |
			=> its 
				->Size=0
				->Capacity=10
				
	->Add elements in arraylist
			
		arra=>	| 8  | 9  | 3  |	 5  |	   |		|   |  |   |	   |
			=> its 
				->Size=4
				->Capacity=10
				
	->Check if full: when size == capacity, the array cannot take another element.
	
		arra=>	| 8  | 9  | 3  |	 5  |	8  |	 3 | 8  | 6 | 33  |	5   |
			=> its 
				->Size=10
				->Capacity=10
		=>So Resize: calculate the new capacity.
		=>New Capacity = Old Capacity + (Old Capacity / 2)
			Example: 10 + (10 / 2) = 15
			
	->Create a new array of the new capacity.
		
			
				NewArray=>	|   |   |   |	   |  	|	|   |  |   |	   |   |		|   |  |   |	
			=> its 
				->Size=0
				->Capacity=15
				
	->Copy all elements from the old array to the new array. This takes O(n) time.
			NewArray=>	| 8  | 9  | 3  |	 5  |	8  |	 3 | 8  | 6 | 33  |	5   |  |		|   |  |   |	
	
	->Update reference: the old array reference now points to the new array.
	 			arra=>	| 8  | 9  | 3  |	 5  |	8  |	 3 | 8  | 6 | 33  |	5   |  |		|   |  |   |	
	 
	->The old array has no reference left, so it becomes eligible for Garbage Collection.
	->Add the new element in the new array.



5. Java ArrayList: Important Properties
	->Default capacity: 10. An empty new ArrayList<>() starts with an empty internal array, and the capacity becomes 10 when the first element is added.
	->Growth: new capacity = old + (old / 2)
	->Stores objects only. Primitives use wrapper classes (int → Integer), which is called autoboxing.
	->Allows duplicates and null.
	->Maintains insertion order.
	->Index based, with fast random access.
	->Fail-fast iterator: if the list is modified during iteration (except through the iterator), it throws ConcurrentModificationException.
	->Generics supported: ArrayList<String>.

	-> constructors 

	ArrayList<Integer> a = new ArrayList<>();        // default
	ArrayList<Integer> b = new ArrayList<>(50);      // initial capacity 50
	ArrayList<Integer> c = new ArrayList<>(a);       // copy from another collection
	
	-> methods

	a.add(10);          // add at end
	a.add(0, 5);        // insert at index
	a.get(1);           // access
	a.set(1, 99);       // update
	a.remove(0);        // remove by index
	a.remove(Integer.valueOf(99));  // remove by value
	a.contains(10);     // search
	a.indexOf(10);      // first index
	a.size();           // number of elements
	a.isEmpty();        // check empty
	a.clear();          // remove all
	a.ensureCapacity(100);  // reserve capacity in advance
	a.trimToSize();     // reduce capacity to size

->Common mistake: for ArrayList<Integer>, list.remove(1) removes the element at index 1, not the value 1. To remove by value, use list.remove(Integer.valueOf(1)).

6. 	ArrayList vs Array

Point			Array						ArrayList
Size				Fixed						Dynamic
Data type		Primitives and objects		Objects only
Length			arr.length					list.size()
Access			arr[i]						list.get(i)
Generics			No							Yes


7. ArrayList vs String
Point			String					ArrayList
Stores			Only characters			Any objects
Mutability	    Immutable				Mutable
Size				Fixed once created		Dynamic
Length			length()					size()
Access			charAt(i)				get(i)
Memory			String Constant Pool		Heap
Package			java.lang				java.util



8. ArrayList vs LinkedList

Point					ArrayList	LinkedList
Internal structure		Dynamic array	Doubly linked list
Random access (get)		O(1)				O(n)

9. Advantages
	->Dynamic size.
	->Fast random access, O(1).
	->Easy to implement and use.
	->Better cache locality, because elements are in continuous memory.
	->Many built-in methods.
14. Disadvantages
	
	->Insertion and deletion at the start or middle are expensive because elements must be shifted.
	->Resizing requires copying all elements, which is expensive.
	->It can have some unused allocated memory (capacity is greater than size).
	->Stores only objects, so there is boxing and unboxing overhead.
	->Not thread-safe.
	
10. Applications
	->Storing a list of records whose count is not known in advance (students, products).
	->Implementing stacks, queues and other data structures.
	->Dynamic lists in programs that mostly read data by index.
